Now back to the show.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.040
While treated like a
contemporary phenomenon,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.460
hustle culture actually has
roots in the late 1800s.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.610
At the time, many folks
suffered from burnout.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.360
That's in part because many of them

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.920
were working much longer hours

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.840
due to the advent of electricity

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.370
and the pressures of the
industrial revolution.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.560
Some of these folks were even diagnosed

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.030
with a medical condition
then known as neurasthenia.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.520
Its symptoms were depression,
insomnia, fatigue,

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.670
and other mental and physical ailments

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.760
which we now associate with
millennial and Gen-Z burnout.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:01.770
But here's the thing:

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:05.800
getting a neurasthenia diagnosis
wasn't strictly negative.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.600
It meant you were faithfully conforming

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.870
to the idealized Protestant
work ethic of the time.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.200
These symptoms were
considered a natural part

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.650
of having an active mind,

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.970
and being a good, hard-working American.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.090
In fact, an alternative
name was American-itis.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:25.620
Naturally, miracle cures like
Rexall's American-itis Elixir

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.890
were marketed to the anxious patients.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.670
Those suffering from American-itis

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.600
were given treatment
recommendations like self-care.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.560
It's like how we're told that
a few minutes of meditation

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.550
each morning will cure our
own modern American-itis.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.510
Still there was one big caveat:

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.300
This disease wasn't for everyone.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.970
The man who coined the term, George Beard,

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.520
believed that Black and Indigenous people

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.680
could not suffer from neurasthenia,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.470
because they didn't overuse their minds.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:54.303
He thought the same

00:04:54.303 --> 00:04:56.430
about working-class Americans in general.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.470
The diagnosis was basically exclusive

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.420
to white, middle-class Americans,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.350
who were supposedly
over-using their brain power

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.230
to pursue the American dream.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:06.850
Which was all well and good,

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.070
but maybe a little elixir
could take the edge off, right?

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.310
In contrast, for marginalized groups,

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:13.890
working to the point of exhaustion

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.080
wasn't seen as a treatable condition.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.440
It was an expectation,

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.900
the only way of achieving
social and economic mobility.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.970
This is how the concept of the hustle

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.840
emerged in Black communities.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.670
The hustle wasn't an option;
it was a requirement.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.560
If Black folks weren't hustling,

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.500
they were even stereotyped
as straight-up lazy.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.400
There was tons of pressure
to be the model minority,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:39.170
miraculously succeeding in
a system rigged against you.

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:42.640
Keeping up with the hustle
was, and has remained,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.550
a matter of literal survival
for Black Americans.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:48.150
It's only recently that
the term hustle culture

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.220
has become commonly
appropriated by white people.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:53.600
But the timing of this
particular appropriation

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.910
over recent years is interesting.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.410
That's because it coincides

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:00.450
with diminishing economic
conditions across the board

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:03.530
for middle- and working-class
people at large.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.250
Today, society applies
the same shaming rhetoric

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:11.430
to practically everybody, though
especially people of color.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.480
If you're cobbling together
a 70-hour work week

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.040
and still not getting by?

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:20.370
Well, the system isn't the
problem, man. You're the problem.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.510
Whether it's called
American-itis or the hustle,

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:24.520
working your butt off

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.180
has long been intrinsic
to the American dream.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.640
Now, that used to mean
working for the same company

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.930
your whole life, rising through the ranks,

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:33.090
and buying a home.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.030
And, of course, earning a retirement

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.670
filled with comfort and Caribbean cruises.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.460
But in recent decades, as
jobs became less secure,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.480
that old-school American
Dream was slowly replaced

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.200
by the ubiquitous ethos of Hustle Culture.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.180
Nowadays, we're told that the
way to achieve true happiness

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.270
and economic freedom isn't
from working hard at one job

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.840
your entire life and climbing
the corporate ladder.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.860
It's from balancing multiple jobs,

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.080
cultivating a unique side hustle,

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.880
and maybe striking gold on a
late-night Doge-coin trade.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.510
But why is this all
happening in the first place?

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.830
That is to say: When did hustle culture

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.870
become so deeply entrenched in our society

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:14.610
that people are getting up at
3:00 a.m. to drive for Uber,

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.370
before a full day of work?

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.440
A lot of it seems to be the
result of one major shift:

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.330
the erosion of labor unions,

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.730
which provide a means
for the working class

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.500
to build collective power.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.310
Since a bunch of shoemakers
first unionized in 1794,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:32.310
American unions have been a
way for working-class folks

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:35.160
to fight back against
unfair working conditions.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.180
During the second Industrial Revolution,

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.170
they achieved things like higher pay,

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.910
safer conditions, and shorter workdays.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.640
Back then, unions fought especially hard

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.420
for a 40-hour work week.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.810
Yet today, high-profile business people

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.037
like Elon Musk tweet things like

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:54.230
nobody has ever changed the
world on 40 hours a week.

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.720
And Silicon Valley employees wear T-shirts

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.820
declaring 9 to 5 is for the weak.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:01.860
Damn! So, what's changed?

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.870
Union membership has
declined, and dramatically.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:08.880
In post-war America, 35% of
the workforce were in a union.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.860
In 1983, that fell to
20%, and by 2020, to 11%.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.450
Some of this is because sectors

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.890
which had high union
density have declined,

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:20.620
says Cornell professor
Kate Bronfenbrenner.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.900
Simultaneously, a high number of jobs

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:25.600
have been created in
newer sectors, like tech,

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.720
where unions haven't existed before.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.720
Union suppression is also rife,

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.800
with companies from Amazon
to Tesla to Starbucks

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:36.120
actively discouraging their
workers from joining unions.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.840
Tech companies in Silicon Valley

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.430
even introduce measures like casual dress,

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:42.930
margarita Fridays and free food

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:46.010
to try and reduce union
membership amongst its workers.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:48.870
But it doesn't stop at
petty attempts to please.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.430
Elon Musk illegally coerced employees

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.747
into not joining unions when he tweeted,

00:08:53.747 --> 00:08:57.680
"Why pay union dues and give
up stock options for nothing?"

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.040
So, what does this decline
in union membership say

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.460
about hustle culture?

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.960
Well, with fewer people collectivizing

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.920
to demand a living wage and better hours,

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.360
we have less control over our income.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.370
Indeed, it's no wonder that
the stagnation of wages

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:15.040
overlaps with the stagnation of unions.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.890
Instead of focusing on the collective,

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.210
we're going at it alone

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:21.720
to try and outpace our
colleagues and climb the ladder.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.780
If working conditions are intolerable,

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:26.900
it's not on our bosses for
denying us bathroom breaks.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:30.350
The responsibility is on us
to manage our own stress.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:32.250
But it's not just the demise of unions

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.040
that has played a role in
promoting hustle culture.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.950
It's also the way overworking

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.090
has been repackaged as proof of worth.

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:42.550
Hustle culture has a way
of changing the narrative

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:46.070
from your boss is a dick
for making you work weekends

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:49.810
to you're doing a great job
if you're working weekends.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:50.860
And if you're not,

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.480
maybe you're just not working hard enough.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.750
Under the prevailing
hustle culture narrative,

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.150
when we rise up the ladder

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.930
due to working overtime or multiple jobs,

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.740
it's not exploitation;
it's a success story.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.340
That's even if the odds
are stacked against you.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.470
If women are having a hard time
succeeding in the workplace,

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:11.230
they can now girlboss their way out of it.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:12.590
- If Angelica's ever going to make it

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:14.510
in a male-dominated power structure,

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.520
she's gotta eat, breathe,
drink and sweat self-esteem!

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.980
- When Yahoo CEO Marissa Mayer

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:23.240
boasted of her 130-hour work weeks,

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:26.890
she wasn't being overworked
by an understaffed company.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.520
She was girlbossing.

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.100
The problem is the
narrative of the girlboss

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.910
takes our attention away
from systemic issues,

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.060
specifically, the numerous barriers

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.320
that women, and especially women of color,

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.190
already face in the workplace.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.950
You know, lower wages,

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:45.120
discriminatory hiring
and promotion policies,

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.230
inadequate maternity leave, and so on.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.490
Instead, our culture
focuses on success stories

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:55.200
of Marissa Mayer spending
18 hours a day at her desk.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.640
And then we wonder why we
aren't doing that, too.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.930
Hustling also presents a way for people

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:04.100
pushed out of the traditional
job market to get by.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.850
Being your own boss is presented

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.530
as a way to navigate systemic racism

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:10.700
and other barriers to success.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:13.780
And both the gig economy and side hustling

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.020
have grown with digital platforms.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.960
Doing casual freelance
work in your free time

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:22.840
is easier than ever via
apps like Uber, Lyft,

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.540
Fiverr, TaskRabbit, and so on.

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:28.670
In this way, recent tech has, ironically,

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:31.610
helped us move away from the utopian dream

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:35.040
that the technology industry
preached in its early days.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:36.990
See, the prevailing notion used to be

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:38.570
that innovative technology

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:40.680
would increase productivity so much

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:43.710
that we wouldn't have to work
nine-to-five jobs anymore.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.950
Hypothetically, we'd all
have a lot more free time.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.970
Now, innovative tech just
seems to find more ways

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:52.780
to fill our free time with more work.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:56.300
This is made all the more
depressing by the reality

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.730
it's really hard to gain
a significant income

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.100
from side hustles.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.610
A survey from Bankrate
found that the average

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:07.270
monthly earnings from a side
hustle were $686 a month.

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:09.860
Notably, though, 49% of respondents

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.340
were making less than $200 a month.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.260
The dream of eventually earning your way

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:15.860
out of your day job, then,

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:18.520
rarely becomes a reality for most folks.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:21.130
But that doesn't fully
explain why hustle culture

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.480
has become so dominant in recent years.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:25.960
One of the things that makes
hustle culture so appealing

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.620
is the notion that by working really hard,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:32.160
accumulating major achievements,
and earning lots of money,

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.420
we will eventually become happy.

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.230
Of course, you can't experience
the joys of high achievement

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.940
and mega-bucks unless
you're being productive,

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:42.650
which is why hustle
culture depends on the idea

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:45.480
of productivity being tied to self-worth.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.030
Constantly working is seen as a positive,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.760
rather than as a sign you're
not being paid enough.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:51.593
- Work's...

00:12:51.593 --> 00:12:53.763
Work's going okay, I would say. Yup.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.180
Working hard.

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:59.460
- One of the symptoms of an
approaching nervous breakdown

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:04.183
is the belief that one's
work is every so important.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.076
That's Bertrand Russell.

00:13:07.076 --> 00:13:08.260
- Huh.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.510
Yes, indeed. Yes.

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:11.343
Yes to that.

00:13:11.343 --> 00:13:13.350
- This leads to toxic productivity.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.480
That's where your self-worth
is so tied to your productivity

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:17.920
that unless you're doing something

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.110
which benefits you financially,
you're wasting your time.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:23.730
When we're valued by
how much we can achieve,

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.790
and especially by how much we earn,

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:27.950
the consequences can be devastating.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.320
If we're not making a profit,

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.530
we're not just economically worthless;

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:33.260
we're literally worthless.

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:36.120
It's no wonder, then,
that job-related busy-ness

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.640
has become a status symbol.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:38.640
In one experiment,

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.360
participants were presented
with a hypothetical man:

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.630
One who either worked long
hours and was really busy,

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:47.500
or one who didn't work
and enjoyed leisure time.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:48.410
As you might guess,

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:51.560
the busy man was considered
more high-status.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.860
But what are the
consequences of this pressure

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:55.240
to never stop working?

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:59.520
We've all heard the
uplifting/low-key dystopian stories

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:01.180
of hustle culture in action;

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:03.920
the Lyft driver who kept
working while in labor,

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:07.160
the girl who sells lemonade
to fund her brain surgery,

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:08.310
and so on.

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:09.540
There's no set answer

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:12.260
as to how often hustle
culture actually works.

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:14.470
A lot of it, as we see from celebrities

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:15.770
and the extremely wealthy,

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.370
has to do with having existing capital

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:20.340
to invest into new projects

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:23.850
or existing fame to leverage
your side hustle off of.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:26.590
That's not to mention being
able to afford nannies,

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:29.110
chefs, cleaners and assistants

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.800
to do the tasks that normal
people have to juggle

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.180
alongside their multiple jobs.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:37.270
The mythos of the hustle
is far less realistic

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:38.980
if you're not already rich.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:40.490
So what about the rest of us?

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:44.390
Well, the stress of hustling
may be reducing our life span,

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:47.970
increasing our risk of dying
from a stroke or heart disease,

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:50.960
and other scary stats you can
Google in the dead of night.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.740
And ironically, studies show

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.320
that working these long
hours and multitasking

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.870
is also reducing our
all-important productivity.

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:02.710
So, we're sacrificing our health,
well-being, and free time,

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:04.710
all in the name of the mythical hustle

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.150
that, at the end of the day,

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.120
probably won't salvage
our bank accounts anyway.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:10.900
So is hustle culture here to stay?

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:14.320
The coronavirus pandemic
was an interesting wildcard.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.410
Obviously, it left a lot of
us working from our bedrooms,

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:19.910
living rooms, and
apparently even bathrooms.

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:21.710
This certainly had its positives,

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:24.710
like greater accessibility
for people with disabilities

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:27.030
and more flexibility for all of us.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:30.440
But we were also expected
to be even more productive,

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:33.010
without water cooler talk to distract us.

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:35.290
So it's no surprise that one survey found

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:38.360
that 68% of remote
workers worked weekends,

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:42.800
and 45% regularly did more
than eight hours of work a day.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:46.680
In the UK, remote workers
have done $33 billion

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.470
worth of unpaid overtime.

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:51.180
But it's not just how hard
you work at your actual job.

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:54.280
Hobbies became commodified
into side-hustles,

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:57.570
especially in industries like
entertainment and journalism.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:01.000
People tried desperately
to supplement lost income

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.150
from being unable to work,
or losing their jobs.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:05.200
So in some ways,

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.170
the pandemic arguably
exacerbated hustle culture.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:10.280
But, on the flip side,

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.160
there's a growing movement
against the notion

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:13.830
that we have to spend our free time

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:15.960
doing profit-oriented work.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:17.460
Hustle culture likes to tell us

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:19.120
that the only reason we exist

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:21.310
is to make money and achieve success.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:23.570
But people are pushing
back against the idea,

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:25.800
arguing that profit-minded work

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.420
is not our only purpose on this planet.

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:31.630
Activists have been promoting
rest as a form of resistance,

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:32.960
asking questions like:

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.700
How will you be useless
to capitalism today?

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.950
Books like "Laziness Does Not Exist"

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.460
remind us that toxic
productivity isn't good for us

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:45.080
and that laziness might
just be a societal concept

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:48.910
invented to make us feel bad
for enjoying our downtime.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.850
Whether this movement will have an effect

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:54.010
on hustle culture at
large remains to be seen.

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:55.700
But what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:58.460
Is hustle culture a way
of motivating people

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:01.980
to live their best life
selling knitted frogs on Etsy,

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.940
or is it a con designed
to make us feel bad

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.440
for spending our free
time watching Netflix

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:09.100
and enjoying junk food?

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:10.820
Let us know in the comments.

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:13.540
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:16.890
Check out our side hustle by
listening to our podcasts.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.260
Hit that subscribe button

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:20.780
like you're clocking in for a 12-hour day.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:22.840
And don't forget to ring that bell.

00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:24.610
And as always, thanks for watching.

